        <section>
          <h3>Summary Post </h3>
          <p>AI writing tools have the potential to transform how written information is produced, from routine administrative tasks to academic and creative 
            writing.  Having considered Hutson's (2021) discussion of language generating AI, I believe these technologies offer considerable benefits, 
            provided their limitations are recognised and appropriate human oversight is maintained.</p>

          <p>>In administrative environments, AI writers can improve efficiency by drafting emails, summarising meetings, preparing reports and organising 
            information.  In laboratory healthcare, this could support the preparation of standard operating procedures (SOPs), staff communications and 
            non-clinical documentation, allowing professionals to dedicate more time to operational and patient focused responsibilities.  AI can also 
            assist with academic writing, brainstorming and improving the clarity of complex information.</p>
          
          <p>However, these benefits introduce significant risks, where Hutson (2021) highlights that language generating AI can produce convincing text 
            without genuinely understanding its meaning.  This creates the possibility of inaccurate information being presented confidently.  
            Bender et al. (2021) similarly discuss how language models can reproduce biases and generate misleading content based on their training data.  
            In healthcare, these limitations are particularly concerning because inaccurate documentation or misleading clinical information could 
            potentially compromise patient safety.  Harrer (2023) emphasises the importance of human oversight when applying large language models in 
            healthcare.</p>

          <p>Creative writing also raises questions surrounding originality, intellectual property and the potential displacement of human creativity.  
            Although AI can generate ideas and assist with written expression, it may lack the personal experiences and emotional understanding that 
            contribute to genuinely original work (Koplin, 2023).</p>

          <p>My peer Mansour also raises a significant point regarding bias and the potential risk associated with machine learning systems exhibiting 
            bias due to training models used.  This is an important consideration to make, particularly when discussing the use of AI in healthcare, 
            due to the potential negative effects that could impact patient care.  Training models may represent a particular race, gender or sexuality 
            in a skewed fashion, and this could pose a risk when implementing to real world practices.  It is important to factor in, and mitigate with 
            human intervention and continuous testing as Mansour has mentioned. </p>

          <p>Overall, I believe AI writers should be viewed as supportive tools rather than replacements for human judgement.  Their outputs should be 
            critically evaluated, fact checked and reviewed before being used, particularly in healthcare environments.  Responsible implementation 
            requires transparency, data protection and clear accountability.  Ultimately, the greatest benefit of AI writing technology will come from 
            combining its efficiency with human expertise, critical thinking and professional responsibility.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <h3>Reflection</h3>
          <p>This discussion allowed me to explore the growing role of artificial intelligence in generating written content and critically consider 
            its applications across different industries.  Reading Hutson (2021) helped me understand how language generating AI systems work and the 
            opportunities they present, particularly in improving efficiency and reducing the time spent on routine administrative tasks.</p>

          <p>I found it particularly interesting to consider how these technologies could be applied within laboratory healthcare.  For example, 
            AI writing tools could assist with drafting communications, summarising information and preparing administrative documentation.  However, 
            I also recognised that their use in healthcare requires particular caution because inaccurate or misleading information could have 
            implications for clinical practice and patient safety.</p>

          <p>Another important area of learning was understanding the limitations of AI generated content, including hallucinations, bias and the 
            potential impact on originality and creativity.  This encouraged me to think more critically about the information produced by these 
            systems rather than automatically accepting it as accurate.</p>

          <p>Completing this discussion also made me reflect how I could use AI within my role as a Biomedical Scientist, working within Haematology 
            and Blood Transfusion.  Consideration towards its usage in the context of SOP writing and document control has given me scope for 
            exploration, and has allowed me to consider how I can be more efficient in the administrative side of my role.  These contributions have 
            been widely received my senior management, and implementation looks promising. </p>

          <p>Overall, this activity improved my awareness of the opportunities and limitations of generative AI.  It reinforced the importance of 
            human judgement, critical thinking and responsible AI use when applying machine learning technologies to real world situations.</p>
        </section>

